WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.040
On the Season 1 finale of The Mandalorian,
our reluctant hero and newest Star Wars icon

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.679
took enough of a tumble to necessitate removing
his helmet and giving us a look at his bloody,

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:10.980
battered visage.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.440
Here's why Pedro Pascal, the actor under the
helmet, looks so familiar.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.440
Game of Thrones

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.010
You might have heard about that plucky little
fantasy program Game of Thrones once or twice

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.589
in the past, but you may not immediately remember
that Pedro Pascal got his own turn on the

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:25.560
program during its fourth season.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.359
He held a recurring role as Prince Oberyn
Martell also known as "The Viper" an utterly

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:33.850
unflappable duelist-slash-poisoner from the
nation of Dorne.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.640
"You shouldn't drink before a fight."

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.210
"You learn this during your years in the fighting
pits?

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.600
I always drink before a fight."

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.680
Despite appearing on only seven episodes of
Game of Thrones, Pascal's footprint is still

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.200
legible on the series even after its end,
both for his character's snappy attitude and

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.970
his incredibly gruesome death at the hands
of The Mountain during a trial by combat.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.390
Playing Oberyn earned Pascal sex symbol status
and before becoming the Mandalorian, it was

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.399
his most high-profile role.

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:04.320
He was recognized as a standout performer
in what is often regarded as one of the best

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.890
overall seasons of Game of Thrones, and his
career only began to soar from there.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:09.890
Narcos

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:13.890
Following Game of Thrones, Pascal landed a
starring role as DEA agent Javier Peña for

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.780
three seasons on the Netflix historical crime
drama Narcos.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.380
The series charts the history of the international
investigation into and eventual capture of

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.250
infamous cartel lord Pablo Escobar.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.430
Pascal consulted with the real-life agent
Peña to properly depict the man's ideological

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:33.970
struggle with the War on Drugs' futility and
the culture of extreme violence and corruption

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:38.530
perpetuated by both the illegal trade itself
and the law enforcement effort made to curb

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:39.530
it.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.740
Narcos might be a more niche selection for
the general Netflix-original-consuming audience,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.270
but the show earned Pascal a large amount
of positive praise.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.670
The series ended in 2017 after its third season,
and was spun off into a Pascal-free follow-up

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:57.460
series about the development of the major
Mexican drug cartels entitled Narcos: Mexico.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.460
Deeper cuts

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:03.100
Like every other starving twenty-something
actor living in New York City in the 1990s

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.630
and 2000s, Pedro Pascal landed a few one-episode
roles on the various versions of the Law and

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:12.520
Order franchise, which you may recognize him
from if you have an incredible memory.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:17.580
Another of Pascal's many TV credits include
a brief recurring role on Graceland, USA Network's

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:22.110
law enforcement drama about undercover federal
agents living out of a confiscated house in

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.110
California.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.910
He appeared only on the first season as an
FBI agent and the subject of an internal investigation,

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.470
who comes into violent conflict with other
characters on the show.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.040
You may also remember Pascal from the sixth
season of The Mentalist, in which he played

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.300
another FBI agent, Marcus Pike.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:38.300
"Beard looks good."

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.130
"It's for an undercover thing."

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:47.270
Turning to lesser-known movies, Pascal appeared
in the 2018 film Prospect – which, curiously

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:52.190
enough, considering his shiny new job as a
Mandalorian, is a sci-fi western.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:56.980
Pascal portrayed Ezra, a prospector for gems
on an alien moon who allies with the teenaged

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.700
main character Cee during a test of survival
in an environment that's both ecologically

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.480
and socially toxic.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.640
The movie didn't receive wide release despite
garnering positive chatter at festivals and

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:12.340
being very well-reviewed on Rotten Tomatoes,
but you can catch it on Amazon Prime.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:18.090
Prior to that role, Pascal appeared in the
2015 comedy horror film Bloodsucking Bastards.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.340
Pascal stars as the evil sales manager Max
— who is also secretly living as a vampire,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.430
to the horrified discovery of his employees
Evan and Tim.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:30.170
This, too, is a movie that isn't exactly going
to enter into the annals of cinematic history,

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:34.620
but "Office Space plus vampires" equals wholesome,
B-movie horror schlock.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.240
The last few years have seen Pascal land roles
in higher-profile movies, including The Equalizer

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.890
2 and If Beale Street Could Talk.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:41.890
Comic book movies past and future

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:45.910
Being a part of the Star Wars universe doesn't
quite count as existing within the superhero

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:51.380
genre, but no worry like most relatively high-profile
actors today, Pedro Pascal's got a couple

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.480
of cape films under his belt.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.521
In 2017, Pascal played the American cowboy
spy operative Agent Whiskey in Kingsman: The

00:03:58.521 --> 00:04:04.500
Golden Circle, the sequel to the 2014 original
that's based on the comic book series Kingsman.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.930
Reviews for The Golden Circle were middling
at best, but just like its first iteration

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:13.300
Kingsman: The Secret Service, the flick is
chock-full of gratuitous slow-mo and cartoonish

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:18.080
violence but this time, Pedro Pascal is there,
sporting a Stetson.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.600
Pascal's next comic book movie role is yet
to come: Pascal will be joining the DC cinematic

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:28.580
universe in June 2020 as one of the antagonists
of Wonder Woman 1984, Maxwell Lord.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:33.180
He's a peak-'80s greed-is-good kind of villain,
and apparently has magic crystals that grant

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.180
wishes.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:38.740
We still aren't sure what Maxwell Lord's end
goal is as a supervillain, but we can't tell

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.060
you how excited we are to see Pascal chew
some scenery.

